---
// Sources: Lunaaaalj/Optimizacion-de-recursos: README.md, reports/model_summary/main.tex
// (formulation, parameters), reports/consolidated/main.tex and reports/resultados_sumario/main.tex
// (context, results of the 8 March 2026 run). Nothing here is stated beyond those documents.
import Project from '../layouts/Project.astro';
import TeX from '../components/TeX.astro';
import SparsityFigure from '../components/SparsityFigure.astro';

const numbers = { demand: 2, calendar: 3, recipes: 4, lots: 5, budget: 6, inventory: 7, terminal: 8 };
---
<Project
  slug="casa-monarca"
  abstract="A mixed-integer linear program that sets a week of food purchasing for the Casa Monarca migrant shelter: which ingredients to buy, on which day and in what commercial lots, so that about 100 residents receive three meals a day within a fixed budget. With the data in the repository, the optimal plan costs MXN 25,995.10, 52% of the budget."
>
  <section class="fig-section" aria-label="Figure 1">
    <SparsityFigure numbers={numbers} />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="problem-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="problem-h">Problem</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          Casa Monarca, Ayuda Humanitaria al Migrante, is a civil-society organisation in Santa Catarina, in the
          Monterrey metropolitan area. It gives people in transit, deportees, refugees and asylum seekers temporary
          shelter, food, primary medical care and legal advice. Food is among its most pressing and most expensive
          needs, and it is paid for from a limited budget.
        </p>
        <p>
          The decision is the week’s purchasing: how many commercial lots of each of 30 ingredients to buy on each of
          seven days. The plan must provide three meal times a day for about 100 people, follow a weekly menu fixed in
          advance, use the stock already in the warehouse and the donations that are scheduled, stay within the budget,
          and end the week with no less stock than it began with.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>The model decides what to buy and when. The menu is set beforehand; the model only has to make it possible to cook.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="form-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="form-h">Formulation</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          Indices run over ingredients <TeX tex={String.raw`i \in I`} /> (30), days <TeX tex={String.raw`t \in T`} /> (7), meal times <TeX tex={String.raw`c \in C`} /> (3) and dishes <TeX tex={String.raw`m \in M`} /> (12). The data are the unit cost <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`c_i`} />,</span> the lot size <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`\ell_i`} />,</span> the quantity of ingredient <TeX tex={String.raw`i`} /> in one
          portion of dish <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`m`} />,</span> <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`a_{i,m}`} />,</span> the portions required <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`d_{t,c} = 100`} />,</span> the menu <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`\alpha_{m,t,c} \in \{0,1\}`} />,</span> the donations <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`\delta_{i,t}`} />,</span> the initial stock <TeX tex={String.raw`I_{i,0}`} /> and the budget <TeX tex={String.raw`B = 50{,}000`} /> pesos.
        </p>
        <p>
          The decisions are the portions of each dish prepared, <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`z_{m,t,c}`} />,</span> and the lots bought, <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`y_{i,t}`} />,</span> both integer. The quantity bought <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`x_{i,t}`} />,</span> the quantity cooked <TeX tex={String.raw`\gamma_{i,t}`} /> and the stock at the end of each day <TeX tex={String.raw`\mu_{i,t}`} /> are continuous and
          non-negative.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>Two kinds of decision: how much of each dish to cook, and how many packs of each ingredient to buy. Everything else follows from those.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Objective</p>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\min\; \Phi = \sum_{t \in T} \sum_{i \in I} c_i\, x_{i,t}`} /><span class="eqn">(1)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>Total spending over the week. Only purchases cost money: stock already in the warehouse and donations are free.</p></aside>
    </div>

    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Demand</p>
        <div class="eq" data-family="demand"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\sum_{m \in M} z_{m,t,c} \ge d_{t,c} \qquad \forall\, t, c`} /><span class="eqn">(2)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>At every meal, at least 100 portions are prepared.</p></aside>
    </div>

    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Calendar</p>
        <div class="eq" data-family="calendar"><TeX display tex={String.raw`z_{m,t,c} \le \mathcal{M}\, \alpha_{m,t,c} \qquad \forall\, m, t, c`} /><span class="eqn">(3)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>A dish can be cooked only where the menu schedules it. The constant <TeX tex={String.raw`\mathcal{M} = 150`} /> exceeds any meal’s needs, so it switches a slot on or off without limiting it.</p></aside>
    </div>

    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Recipes</p>
        <div class="eq" data-family="recipes"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\gamma_{i,t} = \sum_{c \in C} \sum_{m \in M} a_{i,m}\, z_{m,t,c} \qquad \forall\, i, t`} /><span class="eqn">(4)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>What the kitchen uses each day follows from the recipes: portions cooked times the amount of each ingredient per portion.</p></aside>
    </div>

    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Lot sizes</p>
        <div class="eq" data-family="lots"><TeX display tex={String.raw`x_{i,t} = \ell_i\, y_{i,t} \qquad \forall\, i, t`} /><span class="eqn">(5)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>Ingredients are bought in whole commercial units. Eggs, for example, come in trays of 30.</p></aside>
    </div>

    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Budget</p>
        <div class="eq" data-family="budget"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\sum_{t \in T} \sum_{i \in I} c_i\, x_{i,t} \le B`} /><span class="eqn">(6)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>The week’s purchases cannot exceed 50,000 pesos. This single row is the only constraint that links every purchase.</p></aside>
    </div>

    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Inventory balance</p>
        <div class="eq" data-family="inventory"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\mu_{i,t} = \mu_{i,t-1} + x_{i,t} + \delta_{i,t} - \gamma_{i,t}, \qquad \mu_{i,0} = I_{i,0} \qquad \forall\, i, t`} /><span class="eqn">(7)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>Stock at the end of today equals yesterday’s stock, plus purchases and donations, minus what was cooked.</p></aside>
    </div>

    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Terminal stock</p>
        <div class="eq" data-family="terminal"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\mu_{i,7} \ge I_{i,0} \qquad \forall\, i`} /><span class="eqn">(8)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>The week ends with at least as much of each ingredient as it began with, so the next week starts from no worse a position.</p></aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="method-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="method-h">Method</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          The model is written in Pyomo and solved with GLPK, a branch-and-bound solver for mixed-integer programs.
          When it terminates with an optimal status, no cheaper plan satisfies constraints (2)–(8). The run reported in
          the repository, on 8 March 2026, terminated optimal.
        </p>
        <p>
          A Streamlit interface lets the shelter’s staff edit the budget, the portions required, the unit costs, the
          initial stock, the lot sizes, the donations and the menu; solve; and read the meal plan, daily purchases,
          stock, consumption and costs, or download them as text.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two practical features were left out to keep the problem tractable: the expiry dates of perishable
          ingredients and the physical capacity of the warehouse.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>“Optimal” means optimal for the data as entered: prices, stock and menu. When they change, the model is solved again.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="results-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="results-h">Results</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <table class="data">
          <caption class="vh">Indicators of the optimal solution, run of 8 March 2026.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Indicator</th><th scope="col" class="n">Value</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Optimal cost <TeX tex={String.raw`\Phi^*`} /></th><td class="n">MXN 25,995.10</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Budget <TeX tex={String.raw`B`} /></th><td class="n">MXN 50,000.00</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Share of the budget used</th><td class="n">51.99%</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Days with purchases</th><td class="n">2 of 7</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Cost per portion served</th><td class="n">MXN 12.38</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>Cost per portion is the week’s cost divided by 2,100 portions: 100 people, 21 meals.</p></aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r after-table">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          The plan buys almost everything on Monday, 99.1% of the week’s spending (MXN 25,755.10), and the remainder,
          carrots and pasta for MXN 240.00, on Tuesday. Nothing is bought from Wednesday to Sunday. Rice and beans need
          no purchase at all: the 100 kg and 330 kg in stock, with 50 kg of each donated on Monday, cover the week.
        </p>
        <p>
          Five ingredients account for 62.7% of spending: beef (27.7%), bread (11.5%), eggs (9.5%), tuna (7.7%) and
          chicken (6.3%). The heavily used perishables, among them tortillas, eggs, bread and beef, run down to exactly
          zero by Sunday, which constraint (8) allows because none was in stock at the start.
        </p>
        <p>
          The unused 48% of the budget, MXN 24,004.90, could feed more than 100 people per meal, pay for more varied
          dishes, or be kept as a reserve against changes in prices or donations.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>Buying almost everything in one trip is cheapest here because prices are fixed for the week and the model has no expiry dates.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</Project>

<style>
  .fig-section { margin-top: 56px; }
  .after-table { margin-top: 28px; }
</style>
